\name{GoodTillTriggeredOrder}
\alias{GoodTillTriggeredOrder}
\title{A limit-if-touched order that keeps waiting across days until it fires or expires}
\description{
Native Indian stops expire at the end of the day, and this is what brokers sell as GTT for multi-day holdings. A gap through the level fires it at the open, and nothing that watches prices can act on a price that never traded. \code{trigger_price} here is the level, not the order's own trigger. With \code{trigger_on}, the level is compared with the bid, the offer or the midpoint instead of the last trade, or must be reached on two ticks in a row (\code{double_last}) or for \code{hold_seconds} (\code{held}), so a single stray trade does not fire it. It answers HTTP 202 with an \code{outcome} of \code{armed} and sends nothing to a broker until it fires, so keep the \code{parent_id} from the answer. Once touched, its limit is held in UBI's virtual order book, across days until \code{valid_days} runs out, until the other side of the book reaches it, unless \code{hold_limits} is \code{FALSE}; a held one therefore does not die at the close as a \code{day} limit sent at the touch would.

The order template's attributes are described on \code{SyntheticOrder}.
}
